            <sparkle:minimumSystemVersion>26.0</sparkle:minimumSystemVersion>
            <sparkle:hardwareRequirements>arm64</sparkle:hardwareRequirements>
            <description><![CDATA[<style>
:root { color-scheme: light dark; }
body { font-family: -apple-system, 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif; font-size: 13px; color: #1a1a1a; margin: 12px; }
@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) { body { color: #e6e6e6; } }
h2 { font-size: 15px; margin: 0 0 8px; } ul { margin: 4px 0 10px; padding-left: 20px; } li { margin-bottom: 3px; }
</style>
<h2>Asobi 0.9 (build 840)</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>Share This Review</b> opens straight into hosting: the passphrase and one <b>Start Review</b> button, which starts the review for Asobi on your network <i>and</i> makes the web link. The choices that shape the link &#8212; ask for the passphrase, viewer copies, burn-ins, the people list, and a switch to skip the web link &#8212; sit under <b>Options</b>. A review started without a link gets one later with <b>Make a Web Link</b>.</li>
<li><b>Join a Review&#8230;</b> is its own window in the Share menu (and a button at the foot of Share This Review): paste a link, pick a review on your network, or open an invite file. The old Open Invite File&#8230; menu item lives there now.</li>
<li>While a review is live the window reads top to bottom: the link and its passphrase, then <b>People</b> (personal links, with <b>See who watched what&#8230;</b> opening the Review Manager), then <b>Options</b> for the rest, then <b>End Review</b>. Nothing was removed; the visit-by-visit record simply lives in the Review Manager, where it always exported from.</li>
<li>The manual&#8217;s three books follow.</li>
</ul>
]]></description>
